WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.160
What would a conversation look like with zero&nbsp;
questions? This is a bit of an odd question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.280
that crossed my desk. But I have a really fun&nbsp;
answer that has transformed the way that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.600
dive in and engage in conversation with people&nbsp;
both in personal life and also with my wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:26.320
and also in work relationships in a work context.&nbsp;
And it's really interesting. Not that I recommend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.840
diving into a conversation and asking no&nbsp;
questions at all. In fact, me and Will wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.560
a book about How To Ask Questions in order&nbsp;
to create conversations that really matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.600
And yet, if you completely remove questions&nbsp;
from your conversational vocabulary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.840
in this video, I'm going to share 4 levels of&nbsp;
listening that create the opportunity to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.760
a really fantastic conversation even though you&nbsp;
haven't asked necessarily. If you stick around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.600
the video, I'm also going to share 15 phrases&nbsp;
that are really, really phenomenal phrases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.240
that create better conversation, better listening.&nbsp;
These are phrases that can be perfectly fitted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.840
whatever context and content of the conversation&nbsp;
that you're in. And they're really, really useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.880
as reflective listening phrases. So, in a world&nbsp;
where you couldn't ask any questions or for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.040
reason you just were aware of how many questions&nbsp;
you were asking, it started to feel a bit like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.240
an interrogation. These 15 phrases are really,&nbsp;
really useful to pull out of your back pocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.040
to ensure that somebody feels heard and&nbsp;
the conversation continues forward with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:39.840
a little bit of momentum. I'm Chad. Happy to be&nbsp;
in cyberspace with you today. Let's get into it.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:47.360
When I'm watching a video, I love to know&nbsp;
kind of the path of where we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:54.160
before we get strung along too much. So, the 4&nbsp;
levels of listening are verbatim, translation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.960
unstated feelings, and connecting the dots. Let's&nbsp;
take each one of those and expand them a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:07.120
bit. At the end of this video, you will 100% be&nbsp;
a better listener. If for some reason, you feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.880
cheated by this video and you don't feel like a&nbsp;
better listener and a better conversationalist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.440
at the end of this video, you can write me an&nbsp;
angry email. Find my email somewhere and me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.840
will will send you a free copy of Ask Powerful&nbsp;
Questions Create Conversations That Matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.400
Alright. You've got to prove to us though&nbsp;
that you learn nothing in this video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.840
So, verbatim reflection. So, first of all, all 4&nbsp;
of these levels of listening are really levels of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.400
reflective listening. And reflective listening&nbsp;
is the idea that if I were to hold up a mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.640
to your words or whatever you're saying to me,&nbsp;
"I'm just going to hold up and reflect them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.680
back to you." Now, there's lots of ways that&nbsp;
you can reflect. If you've ever been into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.840
fun house at a circus or something, there's&nbsp;
the mirror that's kind of curved that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.440
you look really long, there's one that makes&nbsp;
you look really wide. And then there's just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:58.240
normal mirror. Point is you can reflect in lots of&nbsp;
different ways. First way is verbatim reflection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.480
So, the first way is verbatim reflection. Do you&nbsp;
see what I just did there? Just literally repeated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:10.080
exactly what I just said verbatim. Word for word.&nbsp;
And that is the idea of verbatim reflection. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.560
this sounds ridiculous and when I first&nbsp;
learned it, it felt ridiculous to do. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.560
was shocked. I still am shocked that when I&nbsp;
verbatim reflect what I heard somebody else say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.840
how frequently, they either say, "Oh, that's&nbsp;
exactly what I mean." Or they're like, "No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.320
no, no, no, no." This is what I meant and then&nbsp;
they actually clarify. It's phenomenal. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.680
literally just feeding somebody's words back.&nbsp;
And I think it's partially because we're in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.560
own heads. And as we're speaking, we don't hear&nbsp;
exactly what we sound like when we're speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.280
necessarily. And so, that verbatim reflection&nbsp;
is a really easy way to reflect back. Keep a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.920
conversation going forward. Now, I will say&nbsp;
verbatim reflection is probably most likely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:53.600
end a conversation if the person's like, "Yeah,&nbsp;
that's exactly what I got." And so, if your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:59.600
goal is to have a conversation with by asking no&nbsp;
questions, that might be a tough one. Second one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:09.040
is translation. So, that is where I take most of&nbsp;
your words edit them a little bit and then repeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:15.280
them back. Right? I might take your language and&nbsp;
put it into my language. So, there are 2 types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:21.200
translation. You can do a simple translation and&nbsp;
a far out translation. Simple translation. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.840
making some slight tweaks so that it doesn't sound&nbsp;
like I just parroted back exactly what you said.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:31.280
Far out translation is where I'm using my own&nbsp;
words as the listener and expanding on meaning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:39.120
So, if you tell me... If I ask you, "How was your&nbsp;
day?" And you tell me, "It was really good. I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:45.440
a big win at work. And family life is good",&nbsp;
I could say simple translation. Wow, it sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.720
like you had a really good day at work and things&nbsp;
are going really smoothly with your family, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.400
Just a simple translation. Nothing too crazy. Now,&nbsp;
if you said that same thing a far out translation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:02.400
might look like, "Wow, it sounds like your life as&nbsp;
a whole is going really, really well." Do you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.320
how that was my own words... And I even expanded&nbsp;
on the meaning of what you just said in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:15.360
moment. Now, the power of a far out translation is&nbsp;
when you expand that meaning, you often invite a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.080
really thoughtful response from the other person.&nbsp;
Either clarifying or expanding on what you've just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.680
said. Because you've taken some liberties and gone&nbsp;
extended the conversation, extended what they said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.760
a little bit further than what they actually said.&nbsp;
Harder to do but extremely powerful if done well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:38.320
The third level of reflective listening is&nbsp;
reflecting back unstated feelings. And this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:46.640
really useful one that is extremely underutilized.&nbsp;
And what I mean by that is when we speak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:51.680
words come out of our mouth. And most of the time,&nbsp;
depending on who it is, most of the time they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.080
not necessarily filled with emotional words.&nbsp;
They're not... You know, people don't necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.440
say, "I'm really sad right now or I'm really angry&nbsp;
right now." Sometimes we do. But more often than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.760
not, we're describing something that's really&nbsp;
making us really frustrated. But we don't say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:12.080
"I'm angry." Just we see that frustration. And&nbsp;
so, reflecting back unstated feelings would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.360
something along the lines of just looking back at&nbsp;
them and observing, "Hey, I can see that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:24.800
really frustrated right now". In fact, my wife and&nbsp;
I are reading this parenting book together. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:31.600
love the... There was a comic in it that showed a&nbsp;
kid who bumped his head and was crying screaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:38.000
really reacting. And the parent was just... There&nbsp;
was 2 types of parents. One was... And the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.680
common thing that we do with people's feelings is&nbsp;
deny and dismiss them. You see, somebody's feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.080
really sad. You're like, "No, no. It's okay. It'll&nbsp;
be good. Like this is only temporary." Blah, blah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.840
blah. Right? So, you kind of dismiss and deny.&nbsp;
But the idea here was to unpack a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:07:00.160
further to talk with the kid, to get them&nbsp;
to share and unpack why it is that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:05.600
sad and to acknowledge to get them to realize that&nbsp;
sometimes they're not sad. Because especially when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:11.200
a kid is really, really sad, that is all they can&nbsp;
think about, right? They're so absorbed in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:17.440
world. Whereas if you're able to pull out and you&nbsp;
have some unstated feelings reflected back at you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:21.440
it gives you some more awareness and some more&nbsp;
choice over your emotions which is an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:27.120
powerful gift to give to somebody. And then the&nbsp;
fourth level of reflective listening is really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:33.040
really cool, connecting the dots. This is where&nbsp;
you get to take people and connect them to ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:38.480
and connect people to people and ideas to ideas in&nbsp;
the way that you actually listen and feedback. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.640
so, I used to work for an organization called&nbsp;
World And Conversation and my job was to sit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:47.520
down with a group of strangers and talk about&nbsp;
really soft fluffy topics like race, relations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:54.400
long-term conflict, politics, religion, etc with&nbsp;
no agenda other than to understand the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:08:00.480
And one of the neatest things that would happen&nbsp;
in those conversations maybe about 60 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:05.280
into the conversation is there would be so much&nbsp;
conversational data, so much story, so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.840
experience out on the table that people were&nbsp;
sharing that there were just so many dots that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:16.480
could be connected in that conversation. In fact,&nbsp;
I was in one conversation a dialogue with a kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:21.840
who was black grew up going to a 98% black school&nbsp;
in new york city and a kid who grew up in rural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:28.080
North Carolina where I may not have seen somebody&nbsp;
who was black especially at school. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:33.040
they're sitting in the same room in a conversation&nbsp;
with being facilitated by me about race relations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:40.160
And at the end of the conversation, they looked at&nbsp;
each other and said, "Dude, you are just like me."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.840
It was such a powerful moment because and what&nbsp;
sparked that was they actually connected the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:50.640
dots themselves. I can't take any credit&nbsp;
for that aha or that bridge building.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:56.320
And what essentially brought that up was at one&nbsp;
point, somebody was sharing that their parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:02.960
told them when they went out of the house to watch&nbsp;
out for white people and to be mindful of the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:08.800
you were acting around white people. And somebody&nbsp;
else's parents said, when you walk into the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:14.400
be mindful of how you interact around or with&nbsp;
people who are black. In that conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.600
they saw and they actually they saw these dots&nbsp;
being connected. They actually looked at each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:23.360
other and said, "Dude, you're just like&nbsp;
me." And that's the power of really great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:29.040
connecting the dot type of listening, is as the&nbsp;
facilitator or as a leader or as an educator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:34.080
to be able to connect what somebody's talking&nbsp;
about now with what they did talk about the in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.960
the past or what somebody else talked about in the&nbsp;
past or to connect 2 people in the present. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:44.000
having the language of thinking about listening&nbsp;
to connect the dots will make you a really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:48.880
really fun conversationalist to be around. So, now&nbsp;
that you've got 4 levels of reflective listening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.600
It's helpful to have phrases that mix up the way&nbsp;
that reflective listening sounds. Because you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.360
don't want to sound like Dr. Phil that just says,&nbsp;
"I hear you saying. I hear you saying. I hear you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.280
saying." Right? Repeat back. Because when you're&nbsp;
reflective listening too much you can start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:06.720
sound like a therapist or a counselor which likely&nbsp;
if you're watching this video is not your goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:13.440
or your aim. And so, here are a bunch of different&nbsp;
phrases from page 138 in Ask Powerful Questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:19.040
that you can use to start reflective listening&nbsp;
statements. Now, my editors are going to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:23.840
fill up the screen with them so that you can pause&nbsp;
and screenshot them if you want. My invitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:29.600
before I read them off and share them with you,&nbsp;
my invitation is for you to pick out 2 or 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:36.160
that are not your normal vocabulary of listening&nbsp;
and add them to your back pocket. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.640
when you're able to mix it up, even just between&nbsp;
2 or 3, you don't need 15 phrases necessarily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:45.440
But pick 2 or 3 that feel like you that you'd feel&nbsp;
comfortable using in conversation and just have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:50.480
them in your back pocket. It's a really great&nbsp;
way to keep a conversation meaningfully going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:57.120
without questions. And also to make somebody&nbsp;
feel heard right? In the same way that a mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:01.200
makes somebody feel seen, reflective&nbsp;
listening makes somebody feel heard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.200
So, you might be able to say,&nbsp;
"So, you are wondering...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:12.880
You believe... So, you're thinking about...&nbsp;
I hear you saying. So, what you are saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:20.880
is... You seem... I'm getting... You think... It&nbsp;
is important to you... It feels like you are...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:28.240
What I'm understanding is... It sounds&nbsp;
to me like... You sound as if you are...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:33.840
Sounds like from your perspective...&nbsp;
It seems as though..." Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.480
I don't actually know if that was 15 or not but&nbsp;
here's a handful of phrases. If you want more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

